\f1\b \cf0 Flood Duration and Damage\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 NOAA Flood Duration vs Reported Financial Damage for High-Confidence Matched Events: Shows the primary high-confidence duration-damage relationship and indicates that longer flood duration does not produce a consistent increase in reported financial damage.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	8	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Median NOAA-Reported Damage by Flood Duration Category: Compares typical reported financial loss across duration categories. The longest-duration category has the highest median damage, although it contains relatively few events.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	9	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Number of High-Confidence Matched NOAA Events by Flood Duration Category: Shows the number of events in each duration group and provides sample-size context for interpreting damage differences between duration categories.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	10	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 NOAA Flood Duration for High-Confidence Events With and Without Reported Financial Damage: Compares duration distributions between damaging and non-damaging events. Events with reported damage tend to be somewhat longer and more variable, although the distributions overlap.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	11	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Percentage of High-Confidence Matched Flood Events With Reported Damage by Duration: Shows the proportion of events with positive NOAA-reported damage within each duration category. The 3\'966 hour and >12 hour categories have higher damage occurrence rates, although the >12 hour category contains very few events.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	12	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Flood Duration vs NOAA-Reported Damage Among Only Damaging High-Confidence Events: Examines damage magnitude after removing zero-damage events. Even among damaging events, reported losses do not consistently increase with duration, indicating that duration alone is a weak predictor of damage magnitude.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Geographic Impact Differences\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	13	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 NOAA-Reported Flood Damage by County for High-Confidence Matches: Compares total NOAA-reported financial damage across study areas and shows that Miami contributes the majority of aggregate damage in the high-confidence sample.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	14	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Median NOAA-Reported Property Damage by County for High-Confidence Matched Events: Compares typical property damage across study areas. Miami is the only study area with a clearly elevated median, while the other study areas are near zero.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	15	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 High-Confidence Matched Flood Events With and Without NOAA-Reported Financial Damage: Compares the number of damaging and non-damaging high-confidence floods in each study area.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	16	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Percentage of High-Confidence Matched Events With Reported Damage by Study Area: Shows that approximately 60% of Miami's high-confidence events reported damage compared with about 30% in Jacksonville, while the small Orlando and Tampa samples had no reported damage.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	17	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Mean NOAA-Reported Damage per High-Confidence Matched Event by Study Area: Normalizes damage by the number of matched events and shows that Miami has substantially greater average financial impact per event than the other study areas.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Sensitivity Analysis\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	18	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 NOAA Flood Duration vs Reported Damage for High- and Medium-Confidence Matched Events: Shows the duration-damage relationship separately by study area while including both confidence classes, allowing broader matches such as Gainesville to appear in the analysis.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	19	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 NOAA Flood Duration vs Reported Damage for High- and Medium-Confidence Matches: Combines all accepted matches and distinguishes High from Medium confidence. The broader sample introduces additional large-damage observations, but the overall duration-damage relationship remains weak.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Overall Findings and Impact Relationships\

\f0\b0 Across the figures, flash-flood impacts do not appear to be explained by a single variable. Flood duration shows some relationship with the likelihood of reported damage: damaging events tend to be somewhat longer, the 3\'966 hour duration category has a higher proportion of damaging events, and both events in the >12 hour category reported damage. However, duration does not consistently predict the magnitude of financial loss. Even when only damaging events are examined, some relatively short floods produce much larger losses than substantially longer events.\
Annual flood frequency also shows a weak relationship with financial impact. Years with more high-confidence matched floods do not consistently have greater total damage or larger maximum event losses. Instead, a small number of extreme events can dominate annual damage totals.\
Geographic differences are substantial. Miami has both the largest share of damaging high-confidence events and the highest average and total NOAA-reported financial losses. Jacksonville contains both damaging and non-damaging events but substantially lower losses, while Orlando and Tampa have very small high-confidence samples with little or no reported damage.\
The High + Medium sensitivity analysis increases event coverage, particularly in study areas with fewer High-confidence matches, but does not substantially change the main duration-damage conclusion.\
Overall, the results suggest that flood duration and event frequency provide some information about impacts, but neither alone adequately explains financial damage. Individual event severity and geographic context appear to be important additional controls on flash-flood losses.\
Main Conclusions\
