\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Duration and amount of damage:\uc0\u8232 The relationship is weak. Longer floods do not consistently produce larger financial losses.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Event frequency and annual impact:\uc0\u8232 The relationship is also weak. More floods in a year do not automatically mean more total damage or a larger maximum-loss event.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Geographic differences:\uc0\u8232 Miami shows the clearest impact signal, with a greater proportion of damaging events and substantially higher average and total financial losses than the other study areas.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Overall interpretation:\uc0\u8232 Different characteristics of flash flooding relate to impacts in different ways. Duration may influence whether damage occurs, but the size of the loss appears to depend more strongly on individual event severity and geographic context.\
